-- title:
--   The full Fricke involution equals its own inverse
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a natural number, assumed nonzero. Write $F_N =$ `modularFunctionFieldFull N` for the intermediate field of the field $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ of Laurent series over $\mathbb{Q}$ obtained by adjoining to $\mathbb{Q}$ the family `divisorExpansions N` of $q$-expansions indexed by the divisors of $N$, and let $\sigma =$ `frickeInvolutionFull N` be the $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra automorphism of $F_N$ defined as follows: if there exists a $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra automorphism of $F_N$ satisfying the predicate `IsFrickeAutFull N`, namely one carrying, for every factorisation $a\cdot b = N$ into nonzero factors, the element of $F_N$ given by the $q$-expansion `qExpand ℚ a jq` (the substitution $q \mapsto q^{a}$ in the $j$-expansion) to the element given by `qExpand ℚ b jq`, then $\sigma$ is a chosen such automorphism; otherwise $\sigma$ is the identity of $F_N$. The theorem asserts the equality of $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra isomorphisms $\sigma^{-1} = \sigma$, that is, $\sigma$ is an involution of $F_N$ fixing $\mathbb{Q}$.
--
--   This is the involutivity of the Fricke (Atkin–Lehner) automorphism $w_N$ on the function field generated by the expansions $j(q^{d})$, $d \mid N$, recorded in the form $\sigma^{-1} = \sigma$ rather than $\sigma \circ \sigma = \mathrm{id}$. It is used in the corresponding involutivity statement [`ModularCurve.frickeInvolutionBar_frickeInvolutionBar`](thm.html#ModularCurve.frickeInvolutionBar_frickeInvolutionBar) for the induced automorphism.
